I tried to post this a week ago, but the system crashed as I did... seems it
didn't get through, so here goes again...

Huw Rogers, who ported Nethack 3.0i and 3.0j to the Archimedes, e-mailed me in
response to a query I posted about the absence of Hawaiian shirts. He asked me to
post this message for him, as he has read-only access to the net these days...

He's planning to port 3.1.0 to the Archie, running under an alpha test version
of ARMix, which is a POSIX kernel for the Arc he's just finished. He expects to
release it around April.

Here's some blurb about ARMix from an e-mail he sent me:

...  it looks like a RiscOS application. ARMix processes all run within
that application (which has dynamically resizable RAM allocation). ARMix
supports virtual memory, so resizing just causes more/less stuff to be paged.
The kernel code is RMA resident but invokes an application so as to get at
application memory space. The kernel remaps areas of it's own application
space in order to multiprocess. All user processes run in application space.
ARMix can wimp poll at user specifiable intervals, and the ARMix switcher
timeslice is also user specifiable. By dragging these appropriately overall
system performance can be tuned. The system call interface will conform
to IEEE 1003.1 POSIX.


